# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-release-ships-stale-image — the 1.9.3 -all.zip ships an alfe image older than its source
#+STATUS: WONTFIX
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -

* CORRECTION (clautolisp 1.9.7): the hypothesis below is WRONG

The PROBE crash is NOT a stale image — it is a source defect, now fixed in
[[alfe-autocad-start-engine-malformed-handler-case]]. My reasoning "the source
has no (probe) call" was mistaken: START-ENGINE's handler-case was missing one
closing paren, so its two handler CLAUSES were read as body FORMS and =(probe)=
(the intended condition-variable binding) was evaluated as a call to the
undefined function PROBE. A freshly-built 1.9.3 crashes identically — the image's
age was a coincidence. Compiling the tree emits the exact warning
(undefined function ALFE.BACKEND.AUTOCAD::PROBE); it had simply never been read.

The ONE thing worth keeping from this ticket is the MISSING TEST, carried into
the handler-case issue's acceptance criteria: there is no headless regression
test for the AutoCAD automation *staging* path (print-command-plan --autocad
--mode automation, OS-gated to Windows so it needs the Windows-env mock the
autocad-discover-windows-* tests use). Marked WONTFIX; the substance moved to
[[alfe-autocad-start-engine-malformed-handler-case]].

The original (incorrect) analysis follows, kept for the record.

* Symptom

A clean install of =clautolisp-1.9.3-all.zip= on a Windows + AutoCAD 2022 host
crashes the AutoCAD automation backend with an UNDEFINED-FUNCTION error:

#+begin_example
$ alfe -norc --autocad --cad acad-2022 --mode automation --dwg empty.dwg --print-command
alfe: The function ALFE.BACKEND.AUTOCAD::PROBE is undefined.
#+end_example

It persists after exporting =$ALFE_BOOTSTRAP_LSP= / =$ALFE_RUNTIME_LSP=, so it
is NOT the missing-asset bug ([[alfe-install-missing-cad-runtime-lsp]]) — that
one only removes the two preceding "no bootstrap/runtime LSP found" warnings.

* Why it is a STALE IMAGE, not a source bug

The clautolisp binary in the same install reports *1.9.3*, but the alfe binary
reports *alfe 1.9.0* and =alfe-sbcl.exe= is dated *2026-08-15* — older than the
1.9.3 source shipped beside it. And the source has NO offending call:

- current master AND the =src/clautolisp-1.9.3/= tree on the host both show
  =backend-autocad.lisp= with only =(alfe.error:backend-error (probe) …)= /
  =(error (probe) …)= handler-case clauses — =probe= is the *condition
  variable*, never a function called as =(probe …)=;
- =git log -S '(probe)'= on that file shows only the commit that first added
  the backends — no later removal of a =(probe)= call, i.e. the source never
  had one;
- the whole tree has no =(probe …)= call, no =#'probe=, no =defun/defgeneric
  probe= (only PROBE-FILE and test-scenario PROBE-GRAPHICAL).

So the ALFE.BACKEND.AUTOCAD::PROBE call lives in the shipped *image*, built from
an earlier revision, not in the source the release carries. Same class as the
already-recorded 1.9.0 / 1.9.2 stale-image releases (the archive "reported
clautolisp 1.8.56 and read-autolisp 1.8.0 from inside an archive named 1.9.0").

* What to fix

1. The release lane must dump the alfe image from the RELEASE SOURCE, not reuse
   a compilation-cache image from an earlier pipeline. The release already
   runs each *clautolisp* binary and asserts it reports the source version;
   extend that self-check to *alfe* — run =alfe --version= (and ideally a
   =--print-command --autocad --mode automation= smoke that does not launch a
   CAD) from inside the opened archive and fail the release if the image is
   older than / disagrees with the source.
2. MISSING TESTS (flagged by pjb): there is no headless test for the AutoCAD
   automation *staging* path. =print-command-plan --autocad --mode automation=
   drives START-ENGINE with a mock =:launcher= and =:wait-for-ready nil=, so it
   stages the bridge WITHOUT launching AutoCAD — but the OS gate makes it
   signal :unsupported-os off Windows, so the test must mock the Windows
   environment the way =autocad-discover-windows-*= tests already do. Such a
   test would have caught a PROBE-undefined regression at build time on any host.

* Next step to confirm

Rebuild alfe from the 1.9.3 / master source on the host
(=make -C autolisp-front-end build-alfe-sbcl=, in an environment whose
quicklisp loads — the CI runner's, not the broken =PPBN02261= account) and
re-run the command: a freshly-built alfe is expected to get past the PROBE
error. That confirms the image, not the source, carries the bug.
